<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- NOTE: Xcode's Signing & Capabilities editor rewrites this file and DELETES every
	     comment in it whenever a capability is added or removed through its UI. If this file
	     ever looks bare, the explanations were stripped, not deleted on purpose — restore them
	     with `git checkout` or from the last commit that touched this file. The same reasoning
	     is mirrored in APP_STORE_CHECKLIST.md, which survives those rewrites. -->

	<!-- Universal links for shared .makhtoot projects and template links, so a link opens the
	     document in Makhtoot instead of Safari.
	     binjamshid.com is SHARED with masoon and mahdhar (portfolio namespace:
	     binjamshid.com/<slug>/), so the single AASA at
	     https://binjamshid.com/.well-known/apple-app-site-association must list all three
	     appIDs with DISJOINT path components — /makhtoot/* for this one. That file is not
	     deployed yet; until it is, this entitlement is inert. This is link routing only and
	     introduces no server, no account and no network dependency for the app itself — the
	     no-backend decision is untouched. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.associated-domains</key>
	<array>
		<string>applinks:binjamshid.com</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>iCloud.net.binjamshid.makhtoot</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
	<array>
		<string>CloudKit</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Arrange Mode is the memory-hungry case: a composition where every glyph, tashkeel mark
	     and ligature component is an independently transformable object, rendered at export
	     resolution on iPad. This raises the per-app ceiling on devices that support it and is a
	     no-op elsewhere — a cap, not an allocation. It buys headroom; it does not excuse
	     unbounded retention in MKCanvas/MKRender. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- ⚠️ DO NOT ADD "font-enumeration" TO THIS ARRAY. It has been added twice and
	     broken signing twice, with this exact error:
	         Provisioning profile "iOS Team Provisioning Profile: net.binjamshid.makhtoot"
	         doesn't match the entitlements file's value for the
	         com.apple.developer.user-fonts entitlement.

	     Verified against Xcode's own capability database
	     (DVTPortal.framework/Resources/DVTPortalCachedPortalCapabilities.json): capability
	     FONT_INSTALLATION ("Fonts") maps to profileKey com.apple.developer.user-fonts and
	     defines EXACTLY TWO values —
	         system-installation  ("Install Fonts")      install our fonts device-wide
	         app-usage            ("Use Installed Fonts") use fonts other apps installed
	     The string "font-enumeration" appears NOWHERE in that database. No profile can ever
	     contain it, so it is a guaranteed mismatch. When Xcode reports "doesn't include the
	     Font Enumeration capability" it is echoing this unknown string back as an invented
	     capability name, not naming a real one.

	     Font enumeration — listing the fonts the USER has installed — is a genuinely separate,
	     request-gated entitlement with its own key, requested and awaiting Apple approval. Its
	     key is absent from the capability database above precisely because request-gated
	     entitlements are not published there. Take the exact key from Apple's approval mail and
	     add it as its own <key>, never as a value in this array. Guessing it fails the archive
	     the same way the healthkit .access key did in badan.

	     Only system-installation is declared: that is what MKFontRegistry.installFontSystemWide
	     needs. app-usage is self-serve and already granted on the App ID (the dev profile
	     carries it) but no code reads another app's fonts yet — add it here the day something
	     does, not before, so App Review is never asked to justify an unused entitlement. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.user-fonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>system-installation</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
